(window.CE_PACK=window.CE_PACK||{})["Software Engineering"]=Object.assign((window.CE_PACK||{})["Software Engineering"]||{},{docs:{"assign":"# Software Engineering: Assignments\n\nThree assignments, seven questions each (21 in all), with complete solutions at the end of each assignment. Where a solution quotes a result from code it was obtained by running the code (Python unittest, coverage.py, radon, git); reproduce the experiments yourself and extend them.\n\n# Assignment 1: Processes, Requirements and Design (Chapters 1 to 3)\n\n**Q1. Choosing a process.** For each project choose a process model and justify: (a) software for an infusion pump with fixed regulatory requirements [3] (b) a start-up's mobile app whose features are unknown [3] (c) a payroll system migration with stable requirements and a large team [2] (d) a research prototype with high technical risk [2]\n\n**Q2. Brooks' law.** (a) The number of communication paths for teams of 3, 5, 10 and 20 [4] (b) A project estimated at 60 person-months is late with 6 people: argue why adding 6 more may not help [3] (c) How do small teams and modular architecture respond to this? [3]\n\n**Q3. Requirements.** (a) Rewrite \"the system shall be fast and secure\" as three measurable non-functional requirements [4] (b) Write a user story with acceptance criteria (Given/When/Then) for a member discount [4] (c) Distinguish functional from non-functional requirements with one example each [2]\n\n**Q4. UML class diagram.** Model a library: members borrow books; a book may have several copies; loans have due dates; a member may borrow at most 5 items. (a) List the classes, attributes and associations with multiplicities [6] (b) Mark the constraint and the inheritance (students and staff are members) [4]\n\n**Q5. SOLID.** (a) A class `Report` computes data, formats it as HTML and PDF and sends email. Which principle does it violate and how would you fix it? [4] (b) Explain the Liskov substitution principle with the square/rectangle example [3] (c) Explain dependency inversion with an example [3]\n\n**Q6. Patterns.** Run and explain: (a) the Strategy example (order of 50 with Regular and Sale strategies) [3] (b) the Observer example with two subscribers and two price updates [4] (c) When would you use a Factory? [3]\n\n**Q7. Behaviour models.** (a) Draw (describe) a state machine for an order [4] (b) Describe the sequence diagram for \"member places an order with a discount\" among the actors Member, WebUI, OrderService and PricingService [4] (c) What is the difference between aggregation and composition? [2]\n\n## Solutions to Assignment 1\n\n**Q1.** (a) **V-model/waterfall with rigorous documentation and traceability**: the requirements are fixed by regulation, safety-critical verification is required, and certification needs evidence at each stage. (b) **Agile (Scrum or Kanban)** with frequent releases and feedback: requirements are unknown, speed of learning matters. (c) **Waterfall or incremental plan-driven**: stable requirements and a large team need coordination and a defined plan (agile practices can still be used inside). (d) **Spiral or iterative prototyping**: each cycle begins with risk analysis, and prototypes retire the technical risks early.\n\n**Q2.** (a) n(n - 1)/2: **3, 10, 45, 190** channels. (b) Twelve people have 66 channels against 15 for six; the new people must be recruited, trained and brought up to speed by the existing ones (lost productivity for weeks), the work must be repartitioned (some tasks are sequential and cannot be shared), and communication overhead grows quadratically, so the project can become later. (c) Small teams (about 5 to 9) keep communication manageable; a modular architecture with stable interfaces lets sub-teams work in parallel with few cross-team dependencies (Conway's law: the system mirrors the communication structure).\n\n**Q3.** (a) Examples: **performance**: \"95 percent of search requests shall complete within 2 s with 1000 concurrent users\"; **security**: \"all passwords shall be stored as salted hashes and accounts locked for 15 minutes after 5 failed logins\"; **availability**: \"the service shall be available 99.9 percent of each month (at most 43 minutes of downtime)\". (b) \"As a member I want a 7 percent discount on orders of 100 or more so that my loyalty is rewarded.\" Acceptance: **Given** I am logged in as a member **and** my basket total is 100, **When** I check out, **Then** the discount is 7 percent and the price is 93. **Given** a basket of 99.99, **Then** no discount applies. (c) A functional requirement states **what** the system does (\"the system shall send a confirmation email after an order\"); a non-functional requirement states **how well** or under what constraints (\"the confirmation shall be delivered within 30 seconds\").\n\n**Q4.** (a) Classes: **Member** (id, name, email), **Book** (isbn, title, author), **Copy** (barcode, status), **Loan** (loanDate, dueDate, returnDate). Associations: Book 1 — * Copy; Member 1 — 0..5 Loan (active); Copy 1 — 0..* Loan (one active at a time); Loan * — 1 Member; Loan * — 1 Copy. (b) The constraint \"at most 5 active loans per member\" is shown as a note or OCL (`self.loans->select(l | l.returnDate.isUndefined())->size() <= 5`) and the multiplicity 0..5 on the active-loan association. **Student** and **Staff** are subclasses of **Member** (generalisation, a hollow triangle); Staff may have a higher loan limit (override the limit operation).\n\n**Q5.** (a) It violates the **single responsibility principle** (several reasons to change: data, format, delivery). Fix: separate classes `ReportData`, `Formatter` (with HTML and PDF implementations behind an interface, also giving open/closed) and `Mailer`, coordinated by a thin service. (b) **Liskov**: a `Square` that subclasses `Rectangle` and forces width = height breaks code that sets the width and height independently (`r.setWidth(5); r.setHeight(4)` should give area 20 but gives 16): a subtype must not strengthen preconditions or break the base type's contract; use a common `Shape` abstraction instead of inheritance. (c) **Dependency inversion**: high-level `OrderService` should depend on an interface `PaymentGateway`, not on a concrete `StripeClient`; the concrete class is injected, so it can be replaced by a fake in tests and by another provider in production.\n\n**Q6.** (a) `Order(50, Regular()).total()` gives **50**; with `Sale()` (20 percent off) the total is **40.0**: the algorithm is chosen at run time and `Order` does not change. (b) Subscriber A logs the price, B logs twice the price. After `set_price(10)` and `set_price(12)` the log is **[('A', 10), ('B', 20), ('A', 12), ('B', 24)]**: the `Stock` (subject) does not know the observers; new observers can be added without changing it. (c) Use a factory when the concrete class depends on configuration or input (for example `make_pricing(\"sale\")` returns a `Sale` object) and the client should not depend on constructors, or when creation is complex.\n\n**Q7.** (a) States: **Created → Paid** (on payment) **→ Shipped → Delivered**; **Created/Paid → Cancelled** (on cancel); Delivered and Cancelled are final states; transitions have events and guards (for example payment declined stays in Created). (b) Member → WebUI: placeOrder(items); WebUI → OrderService: createOrder(items, member); OrderService → PricingService: priceFor(total, isMember); PricingService → OrderService: price (the discount applied); OrderService → WebUI: confirmation; WebUI → Member: show confirmation. (c) **Aggregation** (\"has a\", the part can exist independently, shared: a team has players); **composition** (strong ownership: the part cannot exist without the whole and is destroyed with it: an order and its order lines).\n\n# Assignment 2: Implementation and Testing (Chapters 4 and 5)\n\n**Q1. Git conflict.** `app.py` contains `return 100`. On branch `feature` it is changed to 120; on `main` it is changed to 90; then `git merge feature` is run on `main`. (a) What happens and what does the file look like? [4] (b) How do you resolve it and what does the final history look like? [4] (c) Give three good Git practices [2]\n\n**Q2. Boundary value analysis.** A function `letter(score)` returns A for 90 to 100, B for 80 to 89, C for 70 to 79, D for 60 to 69, F below 60, and rejects values below 0 or above 100. (a) List the equivalence classes [3] (b) List the boundary tests [4] (c) Give the expected results [3]\n\n**Q3. Triangle classification.** A function classifies three side lengths as invalid, equilateral, isosceles or scalene. (a) Design tests for all four outcomes and for the invalid cases (non-positive sides and the triangle inequality, including degenerate 1, 2, 3) [6] (b) Give the expected output for (3,3,3), (3,3,5), (3,4,5), (1,2,3), (1,2,4), (0,1,1), (-1,2,2), (2,2,4) [4]\n\n**Q4. Complexity and coverage.** (a) Compute the cyclomatic complexity of `discount_rate` in the notes by counting decisions [4] (b) What complexity did radon report for `letter`, `triangle` and `fizzbuzz`? [3] (c) Define statement, branch and condition coverage with one example showing that statement coverage can be 100 percent while branch coverage is not [3]\n\n**Q5. Mutation testing.** (a) Explain mutation testing [3] (b) With the 8-test boundary suite, what happened to the three mutants (>= 100 changed to > 100, < 500 changed to <= 500, 0.02 changed to 0.03)? [3] (c) A weak suite (50, 200, 1000, a member case and a negative case) gave 95 percent coverage: what happened to the boundary mutants and what is the lesson? [4]\n\n**Q6. TDD.** (a) Describe the red-green-refactor cycle [3] (b) Show the first four tests of a FizzBuzz kata and the output for 1 to 15 [5] (c) What are the benefits and the risks of TDD? [2]\n\n**Q7. Continuous integration.** (a) Explain the CI pipeline in the notes line by line [5] (b) List four further stages of a mature delivery pipeline [3] (c) What is a test double (stub, mock, fake)? [2]\n\n## Solutions to Assignment 2\n\n**Q1.** (a) Both branches changed the same line, so Git cannot decide: it reports **\"CONFLICT (content): Merge conflict in app.py\"** and \"Automatic merge failed\"; `git status` shows `UU app.py` (unmerged, changed on both sides) and the file contains markers:\n```\ndef price():\n<<<<<<< HEAD\n    return 90  # sale\n=======\n    return 120  # new tax\n>>>>>>> feature\n```\n(b) Edit the file to the intended combination (here `return 108  # tax and sale`), remove the markers, run the tests, then `git add app.py` and `git commit`. The history is a merge commit with two parents:\n```\n*   f1d3237 merge feature, resolve conflict\n|\\\n| * ad316c9 add tax\n* | de55bd2 apply sale\n|/\n* 28b2830 initial price\n```\n(c) Small focused commits with meaningful messages; integrate often (pull and rebase or merge frequently to keep conflicts small); review changes through pull requests; never commit secrets or build outputs; protect the main branch with required tests.\n\n**Q2.** (a) Classes: invalid low (< 0); F (0 to 59); D (60 to 69); C (70 to 79); B (80 to 89); A (90 to 100); invalid high (> 100). (b) Boundaries: **-1, 0, 59, 60, 69, 70, 79, 80, 89, 90, 100, 101** (each edge and the value just outside). (c) Results (verified by running): 0 → **F**, 59 → **F**, 60 → **D**, 69 → **D**, 70 → **C**, 79 → **C**, 80 → **B**, 89 → **B**, 90 → **A**, 100 → **A**; -1 and 101 → **ValueError: score out of range**.\n\n**Q3.** (a) Valid classes: equilateral (3, 3, 3); isosceles (3, 3, 5) and permutations (3, 5, 3), (5, 3, 3); scalene (3, 4, 5). Invalid: a zero side (0, 1, 1), a negative side (-1, 2, 2), **degenerate** triangles where the sum of two sides equals the third (1, 2, 3) and where it is less (1, 2, 4), (2, 2, 4) (two sides equal but the inequality fails: it must be invalid, not isosceles: a classic bug). Test every permutation of the sides for the inequality check (a + b <= c, a + c <= b, b + c <= a). (b) Verified outputs: (3,3,3) **equilateral**; (3,3,5) **isosceles**; (3,4,5) **scalene**; (1,2,3) **invalid**; (1,2,4) **invalid**; (0,1,1) **invalid**; (-1,2,2) **invalid**; (2,2,4) **invalid**.\n\n**Q4.** (a) Step 1: Count the decision points: `if total < 0` (1), `if total < 100` (2), `elif total < 500` (3), `if is_member and ...` (4) and the boolean operator `and` inside it (5): V(G) = 5 + 1 = **6**\n(b) radon (cyclomatic complexity): `discount_rate` **6** (grade B), `final_price` **1** (A); `letter` **7** (B), `triangle` **9** (B), `fizzbuzz` **4** (A)\n(c) **Statement coverage**: every statement executed at least once.\nStep 2: **Branch coverage**: every decision outcome (true and false) taken.\nStep 3: **Condition coverage**: every atomic condition takes both values.\nStep 4: Example: `if x < 0: x = 0` followed by `return x`: the single test x = -5 executes every statement (100 percent statement coverage), but the false branch (x >= 0) is never taken, so branch coverage is 50 percent.\n\n**Q5.** (a) Mutation testing makes small syntactic changes (mutants: change an operator, a constant or a boundary) and reruns the tests: a mutant that makes a test fail is **killed**; a survivor shows that the tests do not check that behaviour; the mutation score = killed/total measures test quality (better than coverage). (b) All three were **killed**: the change to `> 100` made 1 test fail (the member at exactly 100), the change to `<= 500` made 2 fail, the change to 0.03 made 2 fail: **score 3/3**. (c) The weak suite reached **95 percent statement coverage** (everything except `final_price`), but **3 of the 4 mutants survived**: the changes `>=` to `>`, `<` to `<=` (twice) went unnoticed because no test used the values 100 or 500; only the constant change (0.02 to 0.03) was killed. The lesson: coverage tells you what was executed, not what was verified; boundary values and meaningful assertions are essential.\n\n**Q6.** (a) **Red**: write a small failing test for the next behaviour; **green**: write the simplest code that makes all tests pass; **refactor**: clean up the code and tests while keeping the tests green; repeat in small steps. (b) Tests: `fizzbuzz(1) == \"1\"`; `fizzbuzz(3) == \"Fizz\"`; `fizzbuzz(5) == \"Buzz\"`; `fizzbuzz(15) == \"FizzBuzz\"`. Output 1 to 15 (verified): **1, 2, Fizz, 4, Buzz, Fizz, 7, 8, Fizz, Buzz, 11, Fizz, 13, 14, FizzBuzz**. (c) Benefits: testable design, a regression suite from the start, small steps with fast feedback, fewer defects found late. Risks: tests written for the implementation rather than behaviour (brittle), false confidence from weak assertions (see mutation testing), discipline needed, less suited to exploratory work and UI details.\n\n**Q7.** (a) `name: ci` names the workflow; `on: [push, pull_request]` runs it on every push and pull request; `jobs: test:` defines one job; `runs-on: ubuntu-latest` selects a clean virtual machine; the steps check out the code (`actions/checkout`), install Python 3.12 (`actions/setup-python`) and run all unit tests (`python -m unittest discover`). The YAML parses correctly (name `ci`, triggers push and pull_request, 3 steps). A failing step fails the build and blocks the merge. (b) Linting and static analysis; coverage and mutation thresholds; dependency and security scanning (SAST/SCA); build of an artifact or container image; integration and end-to-end tests in a staging environment; deployment (continuous delivery with an approval gate or continuous deployment); smoke tests and monitoring with automatic rollback. (c) A **test double** replaces a real dependency: a **stub** returns canned answers; a **mock** records calls and verifies interactions; a **fake** is a simplified working implementation (an in-memory database); a **spy** wraps the real object and records calls. They make unit tests fast and deterministic.\n\n# Assignment 3: Project Management and Quality (Chapters 6 and 7)\n\n**Q1. COCOMO.** Basic COCOMO: organic a = 2.4, b = 1.05, c = 2.5, d = 0.38; semi-detached a = 3.0, b = 1.12, d = 0.35; embedded a = 3.6, b = 1.20, d = 0.32. For 32 KLOC find the effort, development time and average staff in each mode [6]; compare organic 10 and 100 KLOC and explain the exponent [4]\n\n**Q2. Function points.** A system has 10 external inputs (weight 4), 8 outputs (5), 6 inquiries (4), 5 internal files (10) and 2 external interface files (7), with a value-adjustment factor of 1.00. Find the function points and the size in lines at 50 lines per FP [10]\n\n**Q3. Critical path.** Activities: A 3 days (no predecessor), B 4 (after A), C 2 (after A), D 5 (after B), E 3 (after B and C), F 2 (after D and E). Compute the earliest and latest times, the floats, the critical path and the project duration [10]\n\n**Q4. Earned value.** BAC = 100,000; planned value 40,000; earned value 35,000; actual cost 45,000. Compute CV, SV, CPI, SPI, EAC, ETC and VAC and interpret [10]\n\n**Q5. Reliability and availability.** (a) Availability for MTBF 1000 h and MTTR 2 h [2] (b) The yearly downtime for 99, 99.9, 99.99 and 99.999 percent [4] (c) Reliability of a series system of 0.95, 0.9 and 0.99, of two parallel 0.9 components and of triple modular redundancy with R = 0.9 [4]\n\n**Q6. Risk and debt.** (a) Build a risk table with probability, impact and exposure for three project risks and a response for each [5] (b) Define technical debt and give three ways to reduce it [3] (c) Name the four types of maintenance with an example each [2]\n\n**Q7. Ethics and professional practice.** (a) State three principles of the ACM/IEEE code of ethics [3] (b) Discuss the lesson of the Therac-25 or Ariane 5 failure [4] (c) A manager asks you to ship with a known safety defect to meet the deadline: what do you do? [3]\n\n## Solutions to Assignment 3\n\n**Q1.** Effort = a K^b; time = c Effort^d; staff = effort/time. 32 KLOC: **organic**: effort **91.3 PM**, time **13.9 months**, staff **6.6**. **Semi-detached**: **145.5 PM**, **14.3 months**, **10.2** people. **Embedded**: **230.4 PM**, **14.3 months**, **16.2** people. Organic 10 KLOC: **26.9 PM**; 100 KLOC: **302.1 PM**: ten times the size needs **11.2 times** the effort (10^1.05). The exponent above 1 models **diseconomies of scale**: communication, integration and coordination costs grow faster than size.\n\n**Q2.** Step 1: Unadjusted FP = 10 × 4 + 8 × 5 + 6 × 4 + 5 × 10 + 2 × 7 = 40 + 40 + 24 + 50 + 14 = **168**.\nStep 2: Adjusted FP = 168 × 1.00 = **168**.\nStep 3: Size = 168 × 50 = **8400 lines** (8.4 KLOC; with organic COCOMO about 2.4 × 8.4^1.05 = 22.4 person-months).\n\n**Q3.** Step 1: Forward pass: A ES 0, EF 3; B ES 3, EF 7; C ES 3, EF 5; D ES 7, EF 12; E ES = max(EF of B, C) = 7, EF 10; F ES = max(12, 10) = 12, EF 14.\nStep 2: **Duration 14 days.** Backward pass: F LF 14, LS 12; E LF 12, LS 9; D LF 12, LS 7; C LF 9 (E starts at 9 at the latest), LS 7; B LF = min(LS of D, E) = 7, LS 3; A LF 3, LS 0.\nStep 3: Float = LS - ES: A **0**, B **0**, C **4**, D **0**, E **2**, F **0**.\nStep 4: The **critical path is A-B-D-F** (14 days).\nStep 5: C can slip 4 days and E 2 days without delaying the project; shortening D or B would shorten the project (until E-based paths become critical).\n\n**Q4.** Step 1: CV = EV - AC = 35,000 - 45,000 = **-10,000** (over budget).\nStep 2: SV = EV - PV = 35,000 - 40,000 = **-5,000** (behind schedule).\nStep 3: CPI = EV/AC = **0.778** (each currency unit spent earns 0.78 of planned value); SPI = EV/PV = **0.875**.\nStep 4: EAC = BAC/CPI = **128,571** (if the cost performance continues).\nStep 5: ETC = EAC - AC = **83,571**.\nStep 6: VAC = BAC - EAC = **-28,571**: a projected overrun of 28.6 percent.\nStep 7: Action: investigate the causes of low productivity, re-baseline, cut scope or add control.\n\n**Q5.** (a) Step 1: 1000/(1000 + 2) = **0.998** (99.8 percent)\n(b) 99 percent: **87.6 h** per year; 99.9 percent: **8.76 h**; 99.99 percent: **52.6 min** (0.88 h); 99.999 percent: **5.3 min**\n(c) Series: 0.95 × 0.9 × 0.99 = **0.846**.\nStep 2: Parallel (two 0.9 components): 1 - 0.1^2 = **0.99**.\nStep 3: TMR with a perfect voter: 3R^2 - 2R^3 = 3 × 0.81 - 2 × 0.729 = **0.972**.\n\n**Q6.** (a) Example: **R1**: key developer leaves (probability 0.3, impact 8, exposure 2.4); response: knowledge sharing and pair programming, documentation (mitigate). **R2**: requirements change late (0.6, 6, 3.6): response: iterative delivery and a change-control board (mitigate). **R3**: third-party API unreliable (0.4, 7, 2.8): response: wrap it behind an interface and build a fallback (mitigate/transfer). Exposure = probability × impact; address the highest first. (b) **Technical debt**: design or code shortcuts that speed up delivery now but make future changes more expensive (interest). Reduce it by refactoring with tests, adding automated tests and documentation, fixing the worst hotspots first (by complexity and change frequency), and agreeing a \"definition of done\" that prevents new debt. (c) **Corrective** (fix a defect), **adaptive** (adapt to a new operating system or regulation), **perfective** (add or improve features), **preventive** (refactor to avoid future problems).\n\n**Q7.** (a) Act consistently with the **public interest**; act in the best interest of the client and employer consistent with the public interest; ensure that products meet the highest professional standards; maintain integrity and independence in professional judgement; be fair and supportive to colleagues; keep honest about the limits of your competence and the state of the work; continue to learn. (b) **Therac-25** (1985 to 1987): a radiation therapy machine delivered massive overdoses because of race conditions in the software and the removal of hardware interlocks, with poor testing, no independent code review and dismissal of operator reports: lessons: do not rely on software alone for safety, keep independent safety mechanisms, take incident reports seriously, test concurrency. **Ariane 5** (1996): reuse of Ariane 4 software where a 64-bit floating-point value overflowed a 16-bit integer in the guidance system, causing the rocket's loss: lessons: re-verify reused components against the new operating conditions, handle exceptions properly, test with realistic data. (c) Raise the issue formally and in writing with the evidence and the risk; propose alternatives (a reduced scope, a mitigation, a delayed release); escalate to higher management or the safety authority if the risk to people remains; do not ship or sign off a known safety defect: the duty to the public takes precedence, and following the code of ethics protects both users and yourself.\n","exam":"# Software Engineering: Final Examination\n\nDuration: 3 hours. Calculator allowed. Total: 100 marks.\n- Section A: 20 multiple-choice questions, 1 mark each (20 marks). Answer all.\n- Section B: 8 short questions, 5 marks each (40 marks). Answer all.\n- Section C: 5 long questions, 10 marks each. Answer any four (40 marks).\n\n## Section A: Multiple Choice (20 marks)\n\n**1.** The waterfall model is best suited to projects with\nA) rapidly changing requirements  B) stable, well-understood requirements  C) no documentation  D) unknown technology\n\n**2.** The Scrum role that owns the product backlog is the\nA) scrum master  B) developer  C) product owner  D) tester\n\n**3.** A user story typically has the form\nA) a UML diagram  B) \"As a <role>, I want <goal>, so that <benefit>\"  C) a test case  D) a database schema\n\n**4.** Which statement is a measurable requirement?\nA) \"The system shall be reliable\"  B) \"The system shall be easy to use\"  C) \"The system shall be fast\"  D) \"The system shall recover from a crash within 60 seconds\"\n\n**5.** The open/closed principle says software entities should be\nA) open for modification, closed for extension  B) open for extension, closed for modification  C) always open  D) always closed\n\n**6.** The Liskov substitution principle requires that\nA) subclasses be smaller  B) interfaces be large  C) objects of a subtype can replace objects of the base type without breaking correctness  D) classes have one method\n\n**7.** The Factory pattern is used to\nA) create objects without exposing the concrete class  B) notify observers  C) swap algorithms at run time  D) restrict instances to one\n\n**8.** In UML a filled diamond denotes\nA) inheritance  B) aggregation  C) composition  D) dependency\n\n**9.** In Git, `git merge` of two branches that changed the same line produces\nA) a silent overwrite  B) a merge conflict that must be resolved manually  C) a deleted branch  D) a failed repository\n\n**10.** Equivalence partitioning\nA) tests every possible input  B) divides inputs into classes treated alike and tests one value per class  C) measures coverage  D) checks the syntax\n\n**11.** The cyclomatic complexity of a function with 5 decision points is\nA) 5  B) 6  C) 4  D) 10\n\n**12.** Branch coverage is stronger than\nA) statement coverage  B) path coverage  C) mutation coverage  D) none of these\n\n**13.** A mutant that survives the test suite shows that\nA) the code is perfect  B) the suite does not detect that kind of fault  C) the compiler is wrong  D) the tests are too many\n\n**14.** In TDD the refactoring step happens\nA) before the test  B) instead of testing  C) after the tests pass  D) only at the end of the project\n\n**15.** A critical path activity has\nA) maximum float  B) zero float  C) negative duration  D) no predecessors\n\n**16.** Brooks' law is about\nA) adding people to a late project making it later  B) memory growth  C) availability  D) testing\n\n**17.** A schedule performance index SPI = EV/PV greater than 1 means the project is\nA) over budget  B) behind schedule  C) ahead of schedule  D) cancelled\n\n**18.** Technical debt refers to\nA) money owed to vendors  B) future extra cost caused by design or code shortcuts  C) unpaid licences  D) test failures\n\n**19.** Which type of maintenance adds new features?\nA) corrective  B) adaptive  C) perfective  D) preventive\n\n**20.** A series system of components with reliabilities 0.9 and 0.9 has reliability\nA) 0.99  B) 0.81  C) 0.9  D) 1.8\n\n## Section B: Short Questions (40 marks)\n\n**B1. (5)** Use basic semi-detached COCOMO (a = 3.0, b = 1.12, c = 2.5, d = 0.35) to estimate effort, time and average staff for 32 KLOC.\n\n**B2. (5)** Compute the unadjusted function points of 12 inputs (weight 4), 6 outputs (5), 5 inquiries (4), 4 internal files (10) and 3 interface files (7), and the size in lines at 50 lines per function point.\n\n**B3. (5)** MTBF = 500 hours and MTTR = 5 hours: the availability and the yearly downtime. Reliability of a series system of 0.98, 0.95 and 0.9, and of three redundant parallel 0.8 components.\n\n**B4. (5)** Activities: A 2 (start), B 3 (after A), C 2 (after A), D 4 (after B and C), E 3 (after C), F 2 (after D and E). Find the duration, the critical path and the floats of C and E.\n\n**B5. (5)** List the boundary-value tests (with expected results) for `letter(score)`: A for 90 to 100, B 80 to 89, C 70 to 79, D 60 to 69, F below 60, invalid below 0 and above 100.\n\n**B6. (5)** `letter(score)` has an invalid-range check with `or` and four further `if` decisions. Its radon complexity was 7: explain by counting.\n\n**B7. (5)** Describe what happens when two branches change the same line and are merged in Git, and how to resolve the conflict.\n\n**B8. (5)** Give the red-green-refactor cycle of TDD and the first four tests of FizzBuzz.\n\n## Section C: Long Questions (answer any four, 10 marks each)\n\n**C1. Process, requirements and UML.** (a) Choose a process for a hospital drug-dispensing system and justify [3]. (b) Write two measurable non-functional requirements and one user story with acceptance criteria for a ward pharmacy app [4]. (c) Draw (describe) a class diagram with Patient, Prescription, Drug and Pharmacist with multiplicities and describe a state diagram for a prescription [3].\n\n**C2. Design principles and patterns.** (a) Explain SOLID with one example for each of S, O and D [5]. (b) The Strategy example (an order of 50 with Regular and Sale strategies) and the Observer example (two subscribers, two updates): state the outputs and the purpose of each pattern [5].\n\n**C3. Testing.** (a) Design tests for a triangle classifier (invalid, equilateral, isosceles, scalene) and give the expected results for (3,3,3), (3,3,5), (3,4,5), (1,2,3), (2,2,4) [4]. (b) Explain statement versus branch coverage and cyclomatic complexity with `discount_rate` (V(G) = 6) [3]. (c) A suite with 95 percent statement coverage lets 3 of 4 boundary mutants survive while a boundary suite kills 3 of 3: interpret [3].\n\n**C4. Project management.** (a) COCOMO organic and embedded for 32 KLOC: effort, time and staff, and the effect of the exponent for organic 10 versus 100 KLOC [3]. (b) Critical path method for A 3, B 4 (after A), C 2 (after A), D 5 (after B), E 3 (after B and C), F 2 (after D and E): duration, critical path and floats [4]. (c) Earned value: BAC 100,000, PV 40,000, EV 35,000, AC 45,000: CV, SV, CPI, SPI, EAC and interpretation [3].\n\n**C5. Quality, reliability and ethics.** (a) Availability and yearly downtime for 99, 99.9, 99.99 and 99.999 percent [3]. (b) Series, parallel and TMR reliabilities for R = 0.9 components [3]. (c) Technical debt and the four maintenance types [2]. (d) A manager asks you to ship with a known safety defect: respond with reference to the code of ethics and one historical failure [2].\n\n---\n\n# Marking Scheme and Complete Solutions\n\n## Section A answers\n1 **B** · 2 **C** · 3 **B** · 4 **D** · 5 **B** · 6 **C** · 7 **A** · 8 **C** · 9 **B** · 10 **B** · 11 **B** · 12 **A** · 13 **B** · 14 **C** · 15 **B** · 16 **A** · 17 **C** · 18 **B** · 19 **C** · 20 **B**\n\nBrief reasons: 4. It has a number and a time limit. 11. V(G) = decisions + 1 = 6. 17. SPI above 1 means more value earned than planned. 20. 0.9 × 0.9 = 0.81.\n\n## Section B solutions\n\n**B1.** Effort = 3.0 × 32^1.12 = **145.5 person-months**; time = 2.5 × 145.5^0.35 = **14.3 months**; average staff = 145.5/14.3 = **10.2**.\n\n**B2.** UFP = 12 × 4 + 6 × 5 + 5 × 4 + 4 × 10 + 3 × 7 = 48 + 30 + 20 + 40 + 21 = **159 FP**; size = 159 × 50 = **7950 lines**.\n\n**B3.** Step 1: Availability = 500/505 = **0.990** (99.01 percent); downtime = (1 - 0.990099) × 8760 = **86.7 h per year**.\nStep 2: Series: 0.98 × 0.95 × 0.9 = **0.838**.\nStep 3: Three parallel 0.8 components: 1 - (0.2)^3 = **0.992**.\n\n**B4.** Step 1: Forward pass: A 0 to 2; B 2 to 5; C 2 to 4; D max(5, 4) = 5 to 9; E 4 to 7; F max(9, 7) = 9 to 11.\nStep 2: **Duration 11.** Backward: F LS 9; E LF 9, LS 6; D LS 5; C LF = min(LS D = 5, LS E = 6) = 5, LS 3; B LS 2; A LS 0.\nStep 3: Floats: **C = 3 - 2 = 1**, **E = 6 - 4 = 2**.\nStep 4: Critical path **A-B-D-F** (zero float).\n\n**B5.** Boundaries: **-1** → error; **0** → F; **59** → F; **60** → D; **69** → D; **70** → C; **79** → C; **80** → B; **89** → B; **90** → A; **100** → A; **101** → error.\n\n**B6.** Decision points: `if score < 0 or score > 100` counts as 2 (the `if` and the `or`), plus the 4 other `if` statements (>= 90, >= 80, >= 70, >= 60) = 6; V(G) = 6 + 1 = **7**.\n\n**B7.** Git cannot choose between the two edits: `git merge` stops with \"CONFLICT (content)\", the file shows the markers `<<<<<<< HEAD`, `=======` and `>>>>>>> branch` around the two versions and `git status` shows the file as unmerged (`UU`). To resolve: edit the file to the intended result (keeping the intent of both changes), delete the markers, run the tests, `git add` the file and `git commit` to create the merge commit.\n\n**B8.** Red: write a failing test; green: make it pass with the simplest code; refactor: improve the design with the tests still green. FizzBuzz tests: `fizzbuzz(1) == \"1\"`; `fizzbuzz(3) == \"Fizz\"`; `fizzbuzz(5) == \"Buzz\"`; `fizzbuzz(15) == \"FizzBuzz\"`.\n\n## Section C solutions\n\n**C1.**\n(a) A **V-model/plan-driven** process with strong traceability, formal reviews and verification (the system is safety-critical and regulated), possibly with iterative increments inside; risk analysis for each increment.\n(b) NFRs: \"The system shall return a patient's current prescriptions within 2 s for 95 percent of requests with 200 concurrent users.\" \"Only authenticated pharmacists shall be able to dispense, and every dispensing action shall be logged with user and time (audit trail kept 7 years).\" User story: \"As a pharmacist I want to be warned when a prescribed drug interacts with another drug the patient takes, so that I can prevent harm.\" Acceptance: **Given** a patient taking warfarin, **When** a prescription for aspirin is entered, **Then** a warning with the severity is shown and the pharmacist must confirm to proceed; **Given** no interaction, **Then** no warning.\n(c) Classes: **Patient** (1) — (0 to many) **Prescription** (each prescription belongs to exactly one patient); **Prescription** (many) — (1 or more) **Drug** (via a PrescriptionItem class holding the dose); **Pharmacist** (1) — (0 to many) **Prescription** (dispensed by). State diagram: **Created → Verified → Dispensed → Collected**; **Created/Verified → Rejected** and **Dispensed → Returned**.\n\n**C2.**\n(a) **S**ingle responsibility: a `Report` class that computes, formats and emails has three reasons to change: split into data, formatter and mailer. **O**pen/closed: add a new report format by adding a new `Formatter` implementation instead of editing an existing `if/else` chain. **D**ependency inversion: `OrderService` depends on a `PaymentGateway` interface, with the concrete provider injected, so it can be replaced by a fake in tests. (L: a subtype must honour the base contract; I: prefer small interfaces.)\n(b) Strategy: `Order(50, Regular()).total()` = **50**; with `Sale()` = **40.0**: interchangeable algorithms chosen at run time. Observer: the log is **[('A', 10), ('B', 20), ('A', 12), ('B', 24)]**: the subject notifies subscribers without knowing them (loose coupling, event handling).\n\n**C3.**\n(a) Equivalence classes and tests: equilateral (3,3,3) → **equilateral**; isosceles (3,3,5) → **isosceles** (also test permutations); scalene (3,4,5) → **scalene**; degenerate (1,2,3) → **invalid** (sum of two sides equals the third); (2,2,4) → **invalid** (equal sides but the triangle inequality fails: it must not be reported as isosceles); zero or negative side → invalid.\n(b) Statement coverage: every statement executed; branch coverage: every decision outcome taken (stronger: 100 percent branch implies 100 percent statement). V(G) = decision points + 1: for `discount_rate` the decisions are the three comparisons, the `if is_member` and the `and`: 5 + 1 = **6**, so at least 6 independent paths must be tested. The 8-test suite reached 100 percent statement and branch coverage.\n(c) Step 1: Coverage measures execution, not verification.\nStep 2: The weak suite (values 50, 200, 1000...) executed 95 percent of statements, but because it never used the boundary values 100 and 500 the mutants `>=` to `>` and `<` to `<=` **survived**: the suite would not detect an off-by-one error.\nStep 3: The boundary suite killed all 3 mutants (score 3/3): its tests really check the behaviour.\nStep 4: Conclusion: judge a test suite by mutation score and by its choice of values, not by coverage alone.\n\n**C4.**\n(a) 32 KLOC: organic effort **91.3 PM**, time **13.9 months**, staff **6.6**; embedded effort **230.4 PM**, time **14.3 months**, staff **16.2**. Organic 10 KLOC: 26.9 PM; 100 KLOC: 302.1 PM: size × 10 gives effort × **11.2** (exponent 1.05 > 1: diseconomies of scale from communication and integration overhead).\n(b) Forward: A 0 to 3; B 3 to 7; C 3 to 5; D 7 to 12; E 7 to 10; F 12 to 14: **14 days**. Floats: A 0, B 0, **C 4**, D 0, **E 2**, F 0. **Critical path: A-B-D-F.**\n(c) CV = 35,000 - 45,000 = **-10,000**; SV = 35,000 - 40,000 = **-5,000**; CPI = **0.778**; SPI = **0.875**; EAC = 100,000/0.778 = **128,571**: over budget and behind schedule; the forecast overrun is 28,571.\n\n**C5.**\n(a) Downtime per year: 99 percent: **87.6 h**; 99.9 percent: **8.76 h**; 99.99 percent: **52.6 min**; 99.999 percent: **5.3 min**.\n(b) Series of two or three 0.9 components: two give **0.81**, three **0.729**; two in parallel: **0.99**; TMR with a perfect voter: 3 × 0.81 - 2 × 0.729 = **0.972**.\n(c) Technical debt: shortcuts that speed up delivery now but add extra effort to every later change (interest); repay by refactoring and tests. Maintenance types: corrective, adaptive, perfective, preventive.\n(d) Do not ship: the code of ethics requires acting in the public interest first; document the defect and the risk, propose alternatives (reduced scope, mitigation, delayed release), escalate if necessary and refuse to sign off. Lessons: **Therac-25** (software race conditions and no hardware interlock caused radiation overdoses) or **Ariane 5** (an unhandled overflow from reused code destroyed the rocket): both show the cost of weak verification and ignored warnings.\n","notes":"# Software Engineering: Lecture Notes\n\nSoftware engineering is the disciplined, team-based creation of software that is correct, maintainable and delivered on time and within budget. It covers process models, requirements, design (UML, principles, patterns), implementation practice and version control, testing, project management (estimation, scheduling, risk), quality and maintenance, and modern DevOps practice. The code examples were run with Python (unittest, coverage.py, radon) and git on a real machine, and the project-management numbers were computed with scripts, so the outputs shown are real. Prerequisites: Object-Oriented Programming, Data Structures and Algorithms, and Database Systems.\n\n# Chapter 1: Software Processes\n\n## 1.1 Why engineering?\nTypical failures are schedule and cost overruns, changing requirements, poor quality, and unmaintainable code; most of the cost of software is in **maintenance** (often 60 to 80 percent of the lifetime cost). Two classic observations: **Brooks' law** (\"adding people to a late project makes it later\") because communication paths grow as n(n - 1)/2: 3 people have 3 channels, 5 have **10**, 10 have **45**, 20 have **190**; and \"no silver bullet\": there is no single technique that gives an order-of-magnitude improvement; essential complexity cannot be removed, only managed.\n\n## 1.2 Process models\n- **Waterfall**: requirements, design, implementation, testing, deployment in sequence; simple, documentation-driven, but late feedback and costly changes: suits stable, well-understood requirements (some safety-critical systems).\n- **V-model**: each development phase is paired with a test level (requirements with acceptance tests, architecture with integration tests, detailed design with unit tests).\n- **Incremental and iterative / spiral**: deliver in increments; the spiral model adds explicit **risk analysis** in every cycle.\n- **Agile** (Manifesto, 2001): individuals and interactions, working software, customer collaboration, responding to change. **Scrum**: fixed-length **sprints** (1 to 4 weeks), roles (product owner, scrum master, team), artefacts (product backlog, sprint backlog, increment) and events (sprint planning, daily stand-up, sprint review, retrospective). **Extreme Programming (XP)**: pair programming, test-driven development, continuous integration, small releases, refactoring. **Kanban**: visualise work, limit work in progress.\n- **DevOps**: unify development and operations; automate build, test, deployment and monitoring (continuous integration and delivery); measure deployment frequency, lead time, change-failure rate and recovery time.\nChoose the process by the risk, the stability of requirements, team size and regulatory needs; plan-driven and agile methods are often combined.\n\n# Chapter 2: Requirements Engineering\n\n**Functional requirements** state what the system does (\"the system shall calculate a discount\"); **non-functional requirements** (quality attributes) state how well: performance, availability, security, usability, maintainability, portability; they must be **measurable** (\"95 percent of searches return in under 2 s with 1000 concurrent users\", not \"the system shall be fast\"). **Constraints** (regulations, technology). Good requirements are **SMART**: specific, measurable, achievable, relevant, testable, unambiguous, consistent, traceable.\n**Elicitation** techniques: interviews, observation, questionnaires, workshops, prototypes, analysis of existing documents. **User stories**: \"As a member, I want a 7 percent discount on orders of 100 or more, so that loyalty is rewarded\", with **acceptance criteria** (Given/When/Then). **Use cases** describe the interaction of an actor with the system (main success scenario plus alternative flows). **Validation**: reviews, prototypes, acceptance tests derived from the requirements. **Traceability matrices** link requirements to design, code and tests. Requirements change: manage change through a controlled process with impact analysis.\n\n# Chapter 3: Design\n\n## 3.1 The UML in practice\n- **Use case diagram**: actors and use cases (what the system offers).\n- **Class diagram**: classes with attributes and operations, associations (with multiplicities), aggregation/composition, generalisation (inheritance), dependencies.\n```\n+----------------+   1      *   +----------------+\n|    Customer    |--------------|     Order      |\n+----------------+              +----------------+\n| name           |              | date           |\n| isMember       |              | total()        |\n+----------------+              +----------------+\n                                       | 1\n                                       | *\n                                +----------------+\n                                |   OrderLine    |\n                                +----------------+\n```\n- **Sequence diagram**: objects and the messages between them over time (one scenario). **State machine diagram**: states and transitions of one object (an order: created, paid, shipped, delivered, cancelled). **Activity diagram**: workflow. **Component and deployment diagrams**: architecture and hardware.\n\n## 3.2 Design principles\n**Abstraction, modularity, information hiding, high cohesion and low coupling**. **SOLID**: **S**ingle responsibility (a class has one reason to change); **O**pen/closed (open for extension, closed for modification); **L**iskov substitution (subtypes must be usable wherever the base type is expected); **I**nterface segregation (small, client-specific interfaces); **D**ependency inversion (depend on abstractions, not on concrete classes). Other guides: DRY (don't repeat yourself), KISS, YAGNI (you aren't going to need it), the law of Demeter.\n\n## 3.3 Architecture and patterns\n**Architectural styles**: layered, client-server, model-view-controller (MVC), microservices, event-driven, pipes and filters, repository. Quality attributes drive the choice (a microservice architecture helps independent deployment and scaling but adds network failure and operational complexity).\n**Design patterns** (Gang of Four) are reusable solutions to recurring design problems. Three examples, run in Python:\n- **Strategy**: encapsulate interchangeable algorithms behind an interface. An order priced with `Regular()` costs 50; with `Sale()` (20 percent off) it costs **40.0**; the algorithm can be swapped without changing `Order`.\n- **Observer**: a subject notifies registered subscribers when it changes. With two subscribers (A records the price, B records twice the price), setting the price to 10 then 12 gave the notification log **[('A', 10), ('B', 20), ('A', 12), ('B', 24)]**: the stock does not know who is listening.\n- **Factory**: create objects by name, hiding the concrete class (`make_pricing(\"sale\")` returns a `Sale` object).\nOthers: Singleton (use sparingly), Decorator, Adapter, Facade, Command, Template Method. Patterns are a vocabulary, not a goal: overuse adds complexity.\n\n```python\nfrom abc import ABC, abstractmethod\nclass Pricing(ABC):\n    @abstractmethod\n    def price(self, base): ...\nclass Regular(Pricing):\n    def price(self, base): return base\nclass Sale(Pricing):\n    def price(self, base): return round(base * 0.8, 2)\nclass Order:\n    def __init__(self, base, strategy): self.base, self.strategy = base, strategy\n    def total(self): return self.strategy.price(self.base)\n```\n\n# Chapter 4: Implementation and Version Control\n\n## 4.1 Good code\nMeaningful names, small functions that do one thing, consistent style (a linter and formatter enforce it), comments that explain **why**, error handling, no duplicated logic, avoid global state, defensive programming and assertions, **code review** (find defects early, share knowledge; reviews find 60 percent or more of defects at a fraction of the cost of testing). **Refactoring** changes structure without changing behaviour, protected by tests.\n\n## 4.2 Git\nGit is a distributed version-control system storing snapshots in a graph of commits. Core commands: `git init`, `git add`, `git commit`, `git status`, `git log`, `git branch`, `git switch` or `checkout`, `git merge`, `git rebase`, `git pull`, `git push`, `git stash`, `git revert`. **Branching** isolates work; common workflows: trunk-based development, feature branches with pull requests (review before merge), GitFlow.\nA real merge conflict experiment (commands run in a scratch repository): the file `app.py` returned 100. Branch `feature` changed it to 120 (\"add tax\"); `main` changed it to 90 (\"apply sale\"). `git merge feature` reported **\"CONFLICT (content): Merge conflict in app.py\"** and left the markers\n```\n<<<<<<< HEAD\n    return 90  # sale\n=======\n    return 120  # new tax\n>>>>>>> feature\n```\nwith status `UU app.py` (unmerged on both sides). The developer edited the file to the intended result (108), ran `git add app.py` and `git commit`, and the history became\n```\n*   f1d3237 merge feature, resolve conflict\n|\\\n| * ad316c9 add tax\n* | de55bd2 apply sale\n|/\n* 28b2830 initial price\n```\nConflicts arise when two branches change the same lines; resolve them by understanding both intents (and re-running the tests), never by blindly choosing one side. Good practice: small commits with clear messages, frequent integration, no secrets in the repository, `.gitignore` for build outputs.\n\n# Chapter 5: Testing\n\n## 5.1 Levels and types\n**Unit** tests (a function or class in isolation, with stubs and mocks), **integration** tests (interfaces between components), **system** tests (the whole system against the requirements), **acceptance** tests (the customer's criteria), plus **regression** (rerun after changes), **performance**, **security** and **usability** testing. Testing shows the presence of defects, not their absence (Dijkstra). The **test pyramid**: many fast unit tests, fewer integration tests, few end-to-end tests.\n\n## 5.2 Black-box techniques\nDerive tests from the specification: **equivalence partitioning** (divide the inputs into classes that the program should treat alike and test one value from each) and **boundary value analysis** (errors cluster at the edges of the classes). Example specification: totals below 100 receive no discount; 100 to 499.99 receive 5 percent; 500 or more 10 percent; members get an extra 2 percent on totals of at least 100; negative totals are invalid. Classes: invalid (negative), below 100, 100 to 499.99, 500 and above, each for members and non-members. Boundaries: -1, 0, 99.99, **100**, 499.99, **500**.\n```python\ndef discount_rate(total, is_member):\n    if total < 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"total must not be negative\")\n    if total < 100:\n        rate = 0.0\n    elif total < 500:\n        rate = 0.05\n    else:\n        rate = 0.10\n    if is_member and total >= 100:\n        rate += 0.02\n    return round(rate, 2)\n```\nA unittest suite with 8 tests (below 100, exactly 100, 499.99, exactly 500, member bonus at 100 and 500, no member bonus below 100, the negative input, and the final price) **passed (8 tests, OK)**.\n\n## 5.3 White-box techniques and coverage\nDerive tests from the code structure. **Statement coverage** (every statement executed), **branch (decision) coverage** (every decision true and false), condition and path coverage. The **cyclomatic complexity** V(G) = number of decision points + 1 (= edges - nodes + 2 of the control-flow graph) is the number of linearly independent paths and a guide to the minimum number of tests: for `discount_rate`, the decisions are `total < 0`, `total < 100`, `total < 500` and the compound condition `is_member and total >= 100` (two conditions), so V(G) = 4 + 1 + 1 = **6** (confirmed by the radon tool: grade B, complexity 6); `final_price` has V(G) = 1. As a rule of thumb keep functions below 10.\n**Coverage measured**: the 8-test suite gave **100 percent statement and 100 percent branch coverage** of `pricing.py` (13 statements, 8 branches).\n\n## 5.4 Coverage is not quality: mutation testing\nA **mutation** is a small deliberate change to the code (change `>=` to `>`, `<` to `<=`, a constant); a good test suite should **kill** the mutant (some test fails). With the 8-test suite: changing `total >= 100` (member bonus) to `> 100` was killed (1 test failed); changing `total < 500` to `total <= 500` was killed (2 failed); changing the member bonus 0.02 to 0.03 was killed (2 failed): **3 of 3 mutants killed**. A weaker suite of 6 assertions (values 50, 200, 1000 and the negative case, no boundary values) still reached **95 percent** statement coverage, yet **3 of its 4 boundary mutants survived** (>= to >, < to <=, and < 100 to <= 100) and only the bonus-value mutant was killed. Lesson: high coverage with bad test values proves little; boundary values and meaningful assertions matter.\n\n## 5.5 Test-driven development and CI\n**TDD**: write a failing test (red), write the simplest code that passes (green), refactor; repeat. Benefits: testable design, a regression suite, small steps. **Continuous integration**: every push triggers an automated build and test run on a clean machine (GitHub Actions, GitLab CI, Jenkins); **continuous delivery/deployment** extends it to releases. A minimal pipeline:\n```yaml\nname: ci\non: [push, pull_request]\njobs:\n  test:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n      - uses: actions/setup-python@v5\n        with: { python-version: \"3.12\" }\n      - run: python -m unittest discover\n```\nOther practices: static analysis and linters, code-coverage gates, dependency and security scanning, containers (Docker) for reproducible environments, feature flags, canary releases, monitoring and rollback.\n\n# Chapter 6: Project Management\n\n## 6.1 Estimation\n**Basic COCOMO**: Effort = a (KLOC)^b person-months; Time = c (Effort)^d months. Organic (small, familiar teams) a = 2.4, b = 1.05, c = 2.5, d = 0.38; semi-detached 3.0, 1.12, 2.5, 0.35; embedded 3.6, 1.20, 2.5, 0.32. Results:\n```\nmode           size       effort      time       average staff\norganic        10 KLOC    26.9 PM     8.7 mo     3.1\norganic        32 KLOC    91.3 PM     13.9 mo    6.6\norganic        100 KLOC   302.1 PM    21.9 mo    13.8\nsemi-detached  32 KLOC    145.5 PM    14.3 mo    10.2\nembedded       32 KLOC    230.4 PM    14.3 mo    16.2\nembedded       100 KLOC   904.3 PM    22.1 mo    41.0\n```\nEffort grows faster than size (exponent above 1: diseconomy of scale). **Function points**: weighted counts of external inputs (4), outputs (5), inquiries (4), internal files (10) and interface files (7) adjusted by a value factor: 10 inputs, 8 outputs, 6 inquiries, 5 internal and 2 external files give an unadjusted count of 40 + 40 + 24 + 50 + 14 = **168 FP** (about 8400 lines at 50 lines per FP). Estimation is uncertain: use ranges, historical data, expert judgement (planning poker, story points) and re-estimate as you learn (the cone of uncertainty).\n\n## 6.2 Scheduling: the critical path method\nActivities with durations and predecessors: A (3), B (4, after A), C (2, after A), D (5, after B), E (3, after B and C), F (2, after D and E). Forward pass (earliest start and finish): A 0 to 3; B 3 to 7; C 3 to 5; D 7 to 12; E 7 to 10; F 12 to 14. The project takes **14** time units. Backward pass gives the latest times: **float** = LS - ES: A 0, B 0, **C 4**, D 0, **E 2**, F 0. The **critical path** (zero float) is **A-B-D-F**; delaying any critical activity delays the project, while C may slip by 4 and E by 2 without effect.\n**Earned value management**: budget at completion BAC = 100,000; planned value PV = 40,000; earned value EV = 35,000; actual cost AC = 45,000. Cost variance CV = EV - AC = **-10,000**; schedule variance SV = EV - PV = **-5,000**; CPI = EV/AC = **0.778**; SPI = EV/PV = **0.875** (over budget and behind schedule); estimate at completion EAC = BAC/CPI = **128,571**; estimate to complete = EAC - AC = 83,571; variance at completion = BAC - EAC = **-28,571**.\n\n## 6.3 Risk and teams\n**Risk management**: identify, analyse (probability × impact), plan responses (avoid, mitigate, transfer, accept), monitor. Typical risks: unstable requirements, key staff leaving, technology immaturity, underestimated complexity. Teams need clear roles, communication (Brooks' law), code ownership norms, retrospectives; distributed work needs written decisions and tooling.\n\n# Chapter 7: Quality, Reliability, Maintenance and Ethics\n\n**Quality attributes** and metrics: defect density (defects per KLOC), test coverage, complexity, coupling and cohesion, code churn, mean time between failures (MTBF), mean time to repair (MTTR). **Availability** = MTBF/(MTBF + MTTR): MTBF 1000 h with MTTR 2 h gives **0.998**. Downtime per year: 99 percent = **87.6 h**; 99.9 percent = **8.76 h**; 99.99 percent = **52.6 min**; 99.999 percent = **5.3 min**. **Reliability of structures**: components in series multiply (0.95 × 0.9 × 0.99 = **0.846**); two redundant components in parallel: 1 - (1 - 0.9)^2 = **0.99**; triple modular redundancy with majority voting at R = 0.9: 3R^2 - 2R^3 = **0.972**.\n**Maintenance**: corrective (bugs), adaptive (environment changes), perfective (new features), preventive (refactoring). **Technical debt**: shortcuts that save time now and cost more later (interest = extra effort in each change); repay by refactoring, tests and documentation. **Legacy systems**: wrap, re-engineer, replace (strangler pattern).\n**Security and safety**: threat modelling, secure coding (validate input, least privilege, no hard-coded secrets), dependency scanning; safety-critical software (avionics, medical) follows standards (DO-178C, IEC 61508) with traceability and extensive verification.\n**Professional ethics**: the ACM/IEEE Software Engineering Code of Ethics: act in the public interest, honesty about limits, respect for privacy and intellectual property; examples of failures (the Therac-25 radiation overdoses, the Ariane 5 integer overflow, the Boeing 737 MAX flight-control software) show the cost of poor engineering and weak safety culture.\n\n## Quick reference\n- Brooks channels n(n - 1)/2; COCOMO effort = a KLOC^b; availability = MTBF/(MTBF + MTTR).\n- V(G) = decisions + 1; branch coverage; mutation score = killed mutants/total.\n- CPM: float = LS - ES; critical path has zero float. EVM: CPI = EV/AC, SPI = EV/PV, EAC = BAC/CPI.\n- SOLID; TDD: red, green, refactor; git: branch, merge, resolve conflicts, commit.\n","plan":"# Software Engineering: 14-Week Study Plan and Resources\n\nTime budget: 9 to 11 hours per week (3 reading, 6 to 7 building and practising, 1 review with the app's quizzes). Software engineering is learned by working in a team on a real project: set up a Git repository on GitHub or GitLab, write automated tests from the first week, use an issue tracker and CI, and keep a short engineering log of what went wrong and why. Python (or Java/JavaScript) with a unit-testing framework is enough for all exercises.\n\n## Textbooks\n- Ian Sommerville, *Software Engineering*. The standard university text (processes, requirements, design, testing, management).\n- Roger Pressman and Bruce Maxim, *Software Engineering: A Practitioner's Approach*.\n- Robert C. Martin, *Clean Code* and *Clean Architecture*; Martin Fowler, *Refactoring*.\n- Erich Gamma et al., *Design Patterns* (Gang of Four); Eric Freeman et al., *Head First Design Patterns* (gentler).\n- Frederick Brooks, *The Mythical Man-Month*. Essays that are still true.\n- Kent Beck, *Test-Driven Development: By Example*; Jez Humble and David Farley, *Continuous Delivery*; Nicole Forsgren et al., *Accelerate*.\n- Pro Git (free online) by Scott Chacon.\n\n## Free courses and tools\n- MIT 6.031 Software Construction (free notes); Coursera/edX software engineering and agile courses; \"The Missing Semester of Your CS Education\" (MIT, free) for the shell, Git and tooling.\n- Tools: Git and GitHub/GitLab, pytest or unittest, coverage.py, a mutation tester (mutmut), linters (ruff, flake8, pylint), radon for complexity, GitHub Actions for CI, Docker, Jira/Trello or GitHub Projects, PlantUML or draw.io for UML.\n\n## Week by week\n\n**Week 1: What software engineering is; process models (Ch 1).** Do: Assignment 1 Q1, Q2. Form a project team of 3 to 5, choose a project and agree a process; set up the repository.\n\n**Week 2: Requirements (Ch 2).** Do: Assignment 1 Q3. Write 10 user stories with acceptance criteria and measurable non-functional requirements for the project.\n\n**Week 3: UML and design (Ch 3.1).** Do: Assignment 1 Q4, Q7. Draw the class, sequence and state diagrams of the core of your project.\n\n**Week 4: Design principles and patterns (Ch 3.2, 3.3).** Do: Assignment 1 Q5, Q6. Sit the **Class Test** at the end of the week (Chapters 1 to 5.2). Apply Strategy or Observer in your project.\n\n**Week 5: Version control and collaboration (Ch 4).** Do: Assignment 2 Q1. Create and resolve a merge conflict on purpose; adopt feature branches and pull requests with code review.\n\n**Week 6: Writing testable, clean code; refactoring (Ch 4.1).** Do: refactor a messy function with tests as a safety net; run a linter and complexity tool.\n\n**Week 7: Black-box testing: equivalence classes and boundaries (Ch 5.1, 5.2).** Do: Assignment 2 Q2, Q3. Mock midterm: first half of the Final Exam.\n\n**Week 8: White-box testing, coverage, complexity (Ch 5.3).** Do: Assignment 2 Q4. Measure the coverage and complexity of your project.\n\n**Week 9: Mutation testing and TDD (Ch 5.4, 5.5).** Do: Assignment 2 Q5, Q6. Do a TDD kata (FizzBuzz, bowling, or a pricing rule set) and run mutation tests on your suite.\n\n**Week 10: Continuous integration and delivery (Ch 5.5).** Do: Assignment 2 Q7. Add a CI pipeline to your project with tests, linting and coverage gates.\n\n**Week 11: Estimation and scheduling (Ch 6.1, 6.2).** Do: Assignment 3 Q1 to Q4. Estimate your project with COCOMO and story points; compute the critical path and track earned value for a sprint.\n\n**Week 12: Quality, reliability, risk, maintenance, security (Ch 6.3, 7).** Do: Assignment 3 Q5, Q6. Build a risk register for your project; threat-model it.\n\n**Week 13: Ethics and project delivery (Ch 7).** Do: Assignment 3 Q7. Project: deliver a working increment with a demo, documentation, tests, CI and a retrospective report including measured metrics (coverage, complexity, velocity).\n\n**Week 14: Revision.** Redo missed Class Test and assignment questions, then sit the **Final Exam** under exam conditions (3 hours, formula sheet only).\n\n## Milestone checklist\n- [ ] I can choose and justify a process model and explain Scrum and XP practices.\n- [ ] I can write measurable requirements, user stories and acceptance criteria.\n- [ ] I can draw and interpret use case, class, sequence and state diagrams.\n- [ ] I can apply SOLID and the Strategy, Observer and Factory patterns.\n- [ ] I can use Git branching, merging and conflict resolution in a team.\n- [ ] I can design tests with equivalence partitioning and boundary values, and compute complexity and coverage.\n- [ ] I can explain why coverage is not quality and use mutation testing.\n- [ ] I can practise TDD and set up a CI pipeline.\n- [ ] I can estimate with COCOMO and function points, compute a critical path and earned value, and manage risk.\n- [ ] I can compute availability and reliability and discuss technical debt and ethics.\n- [ ] I scored at least 70 percent on the Final Exam with a formula sheet only.\n\n## Study habits that work\n1. Commit small, commit often, with messages that say why; let the history tell the story.\n2. Write the test first for anything with a rule or a boundary; test the edges, not only the middle.\n3. Treat warnings from linters and complexity tools as design feedback.\n4. Estimate, then compare with what happened; keep the data and improve.\n5. Read other people's code and review requests; most professional growth happens in code review.\n","test":"# Software Engineering: Class Test\n\nDuration: 60 minutes. Calculator allowed. Total: 60 marks. Section A: 20 multiple-choice questions (1 mark each). Section B: 4 problems (10 marks each).\n\n## Section A: Multiple choice (20 marks)\n\n**1.** Which part of the lifetime cost of software is typically the largest?\nA) requirements  B) design  C) coding  D) maintenance\n\n**2.** Brooks' law says that adding people to a late software project\nA) makes it later  B) makes it faster  C) has no effect  D) reduces cost\n\n**3.** The number of communication paths in a team of n people is\nA) n  B) n^2  C) n(n - 1)/2  D) 2^n\n\n**4.** Which process model includes explicit risk analysis in every cycle?\nA) waterfall  B) spiral  C) V-model  D) Kanban\n\n**5.** In Scrum a fixed-length iteration is called a\nA) sprint  B) milestone  C) release train  D) phase\n\n**6.** A good non-functional requirement is\nA) \"the system shall be fast\"  B) \"the system shall be user friendly\"  C) \"95 percent of searches shall complete in under 2 seconds\"  D) \"the system shall be secure\"\n\n**7.** In the SOLID principles, the \"S\" stands for\nA) simplicity  B) single responsibility  C) security  D) structure\n\n**8.** The Strategy pattern\nA) ensures a single instance  B) notifies subscribers  C) creates objects by name  D) encapsulates interchangeable algorithms behind an interface\n\n**9.** The Observer pattern is used when\nA) one object must notify others of its changes  B) objects must be created lazily  C) algorithms must be swapped  D) a class must have one instance\n\n**10.** In a class diagram, composition means\nA) the part can exist without the whole  B) the part cannot exist without the whole  C) inheritance  D) a dependency only\n\n**11.** A merge conflict in Git occurs when\nA) two branches change the same lines differently  B) a branch is deleted  C) the repository is empty  D) a commit message is missing\n\n**12.** Boundary value analysis tests\nA) random values  B) only typical values  C) values at and just around the edges of equivalence classes  D) only invalid values\n\n**13.** The cyclomatic complexity V(G) of a function equals\nA) the number of lines  B) the number of decision points plus one  C) the number of variables  D) the depth of nesting\n\n**14.** 100 percent statement coverage guarantees\nA) the absence of defects  B) 100 percent branch coverage  C) correct boundary handling  D) none of these\n\n**15.** Mutation testing measures\nA) the quality of the test suite (how many mutants it kills)  B) program speed  C) code size  D) the number of commits\n\n**16.** In test-driven development the first step is to\nA) write the production code  B) write a failing test  C) refactor  D) deploy\n\n**17.** Continuous integration means\nA) merging code and running automated builds and tests frequently  B) deploying once per year  C) writing documentation  D) pair programming\n\n**18.** The critical path of a project is\nA) the shortest path  B) the path with the most activities  C) the longest path, with zero float  D) the cheapest path\n\n**19.** A cost performance index CPI = EV/AC below 1 means\nA) under budget  B) over budget  C) ahead of schedule  D) behind schedule\n\n**20.** Availability is calculated as\nA) MTTR/MTBF  B) MTBF/(MTBF + MTTR)  C) MTBF × MTTR  D) 1/MTBF\n\n## Section B: Problems (40 marks)\n\n**B1. Process and requirements (10 marks)** (a) Choose a process model for (i) an airborne flight-control system and (ii) a social-media start-up, with a one-line justification each [4] (b) The communication paths of teams of 8 and 12 [2] (c) Write a user story with Given/When/Then acceptance criteria for a password-reset feature [4]\n\n**B2. Testing (10 marks)** A function `grade(score)` returns \"Pass\" for 50 to 100, \"Fail\" for 0 to 49 and rejects scores below 0 or above 100. (a) List the equivalence classes [3] (b) List the boundary-value tests with expected results [4] (c) What is the cyclomatic complexity of `if score < 0 or score > 100: raise; if score >= 50: return \"Pass\"; return \"Fail\"`? [3]\n\n**B3. Coverage and mutation (10 marks)** (a) Define statement and branch coverage and give an example where statement coverage is 100 percent but branch coverage is 50 percent [4] (b) A suite reaches 95 percent statement coverage but misses the boundary values: explain, using mutants such as changing >= to >, why it is weak [4] (c) Name two kinds of test double [2]\n\n**B4. Project management (10 marks)** (a) Basic organic COCOMO (a = 2.4, b = 1.05, c = 2.5, d = 0.38) for 20 KLOC: effort, time and average staff [4] (b) Activities: A 2 (start), B 3 (after A), C 4 (after A), D 2 (after B), E 5 (after C), F 3 (after D and E), G 2 (after F): find the project duration, the critical path and the floats of B and D [4] (c) BAC = 200,000, PV = 120,000, EV = 100,000, AC = 110,000: the CPI and SPI [2]\n\n---\n\n## Answer Key and Solutions\n\n### Section A\n1 **D** · 2 **A** · 3 **C** · 4 **B** · 5 **A** · 6 **C** · 7 **B** · 8 **D** · 9 **A** · 10 **B** · 11 **A** · 12 **C** · 13 **B** · 14 **D** · 15 **A** · 16 **B** · 17 **A** · 18 **C** · 19 **B** · 20 **B**\n\nBrief reasons: 1. Maintenance is typically 60 to 80 percent of lifetime cost. 3. Each pair of people is a channel. 6. It is specific and measurable. 14. Statement coverage says nothing about untested boundaries or correctness. 19. CPI < 1: each unit spent earns less than one unit of planned value.\n\n### Section B\n\n**B1.**\n(a) (i) **V-model/plan-driven** with rigorous documentation, traceability and certification evidence (safety-critical, regulated). (ii) **Agile (Scrum/Kanban)** with short iterations and continuous deployment (requirements unknown, speed of feedback is critical).\n(b) 8 people: 8 × 7/2 = **28** paths; 12 people: 12 × 11/2 = **66** paths.\n(c) \"As a registered user who forgot my password, I want to reset it by email so that I can regain access.\" **Given** a registered email address, **When** I request a reset, **Then** an email with a single-use link valid for 30 minutes is sent. **Given** an expired or used link, **When** I open it, **Then** I am told it is invalid and offered a new reset. **Given** an unregistered address, **Then** the response is the same message (to avoid revealing accounts).\n\n**B2.**\n(a) Invalid low (score < 0); Fail (0 to 49); Pass (50 to 100); invalid high (score > 100).\n(b) **-1** → error (ValueError); **0** → Fail; **49** → Fail; **50** → Pass; **100** → Pass; **101** → error. (Also a typical value such as 75 → Pass.)\n(c) Decision points: `score < 0`, `or score > 100`, `score >= 50` = 3: V(G) = 3 + 1 = **4**.\n\n**B3.**\n(a) **Statement coverage**: the percentage of statements executed by the tests. **Branch coverage**: the percentage of decision outcomes (true and false) taken. Example: `if x < 0: x = 0; return x`: a single test with x = -5 executes every statement (100 percent), but the false outcome of the decision is never taken (branch coverage 50 percent).\n(b) The tests execute almost all statements but use values like 50, 200 and 1000 instead of the boundary values 100 and 500. A **mutant** that changes `>=` to `>` (or `<` to `<=`) behaves differently only at the boundary, so a suite without boundary tests does not notice it: such mutants **survive**, which reveals that the suite does not verify the boundary behaviour even though the coverage figure is high (in the notes' experiment, 3 of 4 boundary mutants survived with 95 percent coverage).\n(c) **Stub** (returns canned answers), **mock** (verifies interactions), **fake** (simple working implementation such as an in-memory database), **spy**.\n\n**B4.**\n(a) Effort = 2.4 × 20^1.05 = **55.8 person-months**; time = 2.5 × 55.76^0.38 = **11.5 months**; average staff = 55.76/11.52 = **4.8** people.\n(b) Step 1: Forward pass: A 0 to 2; B 2 to 5; C 2 to 6; D 5 to 7; E 6 to 11; F max(7, 11) = 11 to 14; G 14 to 16.\nStep 2: **Duration 16.** Backward pass: G LS 14; F LS 11; E LS 6; D LF 11, LS 9; C LS 2; B LF 9 (D starts at 9 at the latest), LS 6; A LS 0.\nStep 3: Floats: **B = 6 - 2 = 4**; **D = 9 - 5 = 4**; critical path **A-C-E-F-G** (zero float).\n(c) CPI = EV/AC = 100,000/110,000 = **0.909** (over budget); SPI = EV/PV = 100,000/120,000 = **0.833** (behind schedule); EAC = BAC/CPI = 220,000.\n"},mcq:[{"q":"Which part of the lifetime cost of software is typically the largest?","options":["requirements","design","coding","maintenance"],"answer":3,"explain":"Maintenance is typically 60 to 80 percent of lifetime cost.","f":0.025,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Brooks' law says that adding people to a late software project","options":["makes it later","makes it faster","has no effect","reduces cost"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.075,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The number of communication paths in a team of n people is","options":["n","n^2","n(n - 1)/2","2^n"],"answer":2,"explain":"Each pair of people is a channel.","f":0.125,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Which process model includes explicit risk analysis in every cycle?","options":["waterfall","spiral","V-model","Kanban"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.175,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"In Scrum a fixed-length iteration is called a","options":["sprint","milestone","release train","phase"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.225,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A good non-functional requirement is","options":["\"the system shall be fast\"","\"the system shall be user friendly\"","\"95 percent of searches shall complete in under 2 seconds\"","\"the system shall be secure\""],"answer":2,"explain":"It is specific and measurable.","f":0.275,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"In the SOLID principles, the \"S\" stands for","options":["simplicity","single responsibility","security","structure"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.325,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The Strategy pattern","options":["ensures a single instance","notifies subscribers","creates objects by name","encapsulates interchangeable algorithms behind an interface"],"answer":3,"explain":"The correct answer is D.","f":0.375,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The Observer pattern is used when","options":["one object must notify others of its changes","objects must be created lazily","algorithms must be swapped","a class must have one instance"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.425,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"In a class diagram, composition means","options":["the part can exist without the whole","the part cannot exist without the whole","inheritance","a dependency only"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.475,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A merge conflict in Git occurs when","options":["two branches change the same lines differently","a branch is deleted","the repository is empty","a commit message is missing"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.525,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Boundary value analysis tests","options":["random values","only typical values","values at and just around the edges of equivalence classes","only invalid values"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.575,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The cyclomatic complexity V(G) of a function equals","options":["the number of lines","the number of decision points plus one","the number of variables","the depth of nesting"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.625,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"100 percent statement coverage guarantees","options":["the absence of defects","100 percent branch coverage","correct boundary handling","none of these"],"answer":3,"explain":"Statement coverage says nothing about untested boundaries or correctness.","f":0.675,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Mutation testing measures","options":["the quality of the test suite (how many mutants it kills)","program speed","code size","the number of commits"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.725,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"In test-driven development the first step is to","options":["write the production code","write a failing test","refactor","deploy"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.775,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Continuous integration means","options":["merging code and running automated builds and tests frequently","deploying once per year","writing documentation","pair programming"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.825,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The critical path of a project is","options":["the shortest path","the path with the most activities","the longest path, with zero float","the cheapest path"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.875,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A cost performance index CPI = EV/AC below 1 means","options":["under budget","over budget","ahead of schedule","behind schedule"],"answer":1,"explain":"CPI < 1: each unit spent earns less than one unit of planned value.","f":0.925,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Availability is calculated as","options":["MTTR/MTBF","MTBF/(MTBF + MTTR)","MTBF × MTTR","1/MTBF"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.975,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The waterfall model is best suited to projects with","options":["rapidly changing requirements","stable, well-understood requirements","no documentation","unknown technology"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.025,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The Scrum role that owns the product backlog is the","options":["scrum master","developer","product owner","tester"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.075,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A user story typically has the form","options":["a UML diagram","\"As a <role>, I want <goal>, so that <benefit>\"","a test case","a database schema"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.125,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Which statement is a measurable requirement?","options":["\"The system shall be reliable\"","\"The system shall be easy to use\"","\"The system shall be fast\"","\"The system shall recover from a crash within 60 seconds\""],"answer":3,"explain":"It has a number and a time limit.","f":0.175,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The open/closed principle says software entities should be","options":["open for modification, closed for extension","open for extension, closed for modification","always open","always closed"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.225,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The Liskov substitution principle requires that","options":["subclasses be smaller","interfaces be large","objects of a subtype can replace objects of the base type without breaking correctness","classes have one method"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.275,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The Factory pattern is used to","options":["create objects without exposing the concrete class","notify observers","swap algorithms at run time","restrict instances to one"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.325,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"In UML a filled diamond denotes","options":["inheritance","aggregation","composition","dependency"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.375,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"In Git, `git merge` of two branches that changed the same line produces","options":["a silent overwrite","a merge conflict that must be resolved manually","a deleted branch","a failed repository"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.425,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Equivalence partitioning","options":["tests every possible input","divides inputs into classes treated alike and tests one value per class","measures coverage","checks the syntax"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.475,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The cyclomatic complexity of a function with 5 decision points is","options":["5","6","4","10"],"answer":1,"explain":"V(G) = decisions + 1 = 6.","f":0.525,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Branch coverage is stronger than","options":["statement coverage","path coverage","mutation coverage","none of these"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.575,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A mutant that survives the test suite shows that","options":["the code is perfect","the suite does not detect that kind of fault","the compiler is wrong","the tests are too many"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.625,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"In TDD the refactoring step happens","options":["before the test","instead of testing","after the tests pass","only at the end of the project"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.675,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A critical path activity has","options":["maximum float","zero float","negative duration","no predecessors"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.725,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Brooks' law is about","options":["adding people to a late project making it later","memory growth","availability","testing"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.775,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A schedule performance index SPI = EV/PV greater than 1 means the project is","options":["over budget","behind schedule","ahead of schedule","cancelled"],"answer":2,"explain":"SPI above 1 means more value earned than planned.","f":0.825,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Technical debt refers to","options":["money owed to vendors","future extra cost caused by design or code shortcuts","unpaid licences","test failures"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.875,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Which type of maintenance adds new features?","options":["corrective","adaptive","perfective","preventive"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.925,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A series system of components with reliabilities 0.9 and 0.9 has reliability","options":["0.99","0.81","0.9","1.8"],"answer":1,"explain":"0.9 × 0.9 = 0.81.","f":0.975,"src":"Final exam"}]});
